Pneumonia
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Fever with severe rigors, Productive cough with purulent sputum, Pleuritic chest pain, Focal consolidation on chest radiograph

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-035
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of high fever, productive cough, and severe chest pain with breathing, requiring urgent respiratory assessment to rule out severe lobar pneumonia and assess oxygenation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an acute onset of high fever, severe shaking chills, and a productive cough with sharp chest pain. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate respiratory effort, identify focal consolidation, and initiate immediate antibiotic therapy to prevent respiratory failure prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've been shivering uncontrollably all night, my chest hurts terribly when I take a deep breath, and I'm coughing up thick green gunk."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced acutely 24 hours ago with fever frequently accompanied by severe shaking rigors. The patient reports a highly productive cough yielding thick purulent, distinctly rust-coloured, or blood-tinged sputum. The patient describes pleuritic chest pain that is characteristically sharp and exquisitely worsened by deep inspiration. Over the last 12 hours, the patient has developed progressive exertional dyspnoea, significant tachypnoea, generalized severe fatigue, extreme myalgias, and systemic malaise. No sudden onset of acute confusion or profound delirium (patient is not elderly/frail).
Functional Status: Bedbound, breathless upon sitting up.
Social/Safety Concerns: Spouse is present; patient is highly fatigued but currently maintaining airway.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 | HR: 110 | Temp: 39.1 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Acutely ill-appearing, flushed, diaphoretic, taking shallow breaths to splint the chest.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals tachypnoea. Auscultation reveals focal bronchial breath sounds and distinct localized crackles strictly over the left lower lung lobe. Percussion demonstrates localized dullness, and tactile fremitus is increased over this area, indicating dense lobar consolidation. Cardiovascular exam reveals tachycardia out of proportion to the fever, but hemodynamically stable. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Fever with severe rigors, Productive cough with purulent sputum, Pleuritic chest pain, and Focal consolidation on chest radiograph (pending formal imaging).
Environmental Observations: Clear access to bathroom; room is adequately ventilated.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Taking over-the-counter cough suppressants (advised to cease).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Community-Acquired Pneumonia (Lobar) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Bronchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a high fever with rigors, localized dullness to percussion, and focal bronchial breath sounds, which define a parenchymal (lung tissue) infection rather than just airway inflammation.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of high fever, rigors, and frankly purulent sputum, pointing definitively to an infectious process, despite the shared symptom of pleuritic chest pain.

COVID-19/Viral Pneumonitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The strictly unilateral, dense lobar consolidation signs (dullness, bronchial breathing) and purulent sputum, which strongly favor a typical bacterial etiology (like Streptococcus pneumoniae) over a viral, bilateral ground-glass presentation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic clinical hallmarks of acute bacterial lobar pneumonia: high fever, purulent productive cough, pleuritic pain, and focal dense consolidation on auscultation. The presence of tachypnoea (RR 24) and borderline hypoxia (SpO2 94%) on room air indicates significant respiratory compromise. An urgent clinical examination was mandatory to accurately localize the consolidation, quantify the degree of hypoxia, and immediately initiate targeted oral antibiotics (e.g., high-dose Amoxicillin or a Macrolide) to halt the progression of the infection. Delaying this assessment and treatment until the next in-hours period carried a substantial risk of progressive respiratory failure requiring hospital admission, satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe high-dose empirical oral antibiotics (e.g., Amoxicillin 1g three times daily + Doxycycline 100mg twice daily, per local CAP guidelines). Advise immediate cessation of cough suppressants to encourage sputum clearance. Arrange for a plain posterior-anterior chest radiograph tomorrow to formally visualize the focal lobar consolidation and rule out pleural effusion.
Positive Behavioral Change: By actively performing controlled, deep coughing exercises every hour while hugging a firm pillow tightly against the chest, the patient will brace against the pleuritic pain; successfully clearing the thick sputum without agonizing pain will positively reinforce the habit of regular airway clearance, speeding up recovery.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Telehealth GP review in 24 hours to monitor oxygen saturations and treatment response; mandatory in-person review in 48 hours with chest X-ray results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient experiences worsening shortness of breath at rest, the lips or face turn blue (cyanosis), confusion or severe lethargy develops, or the chest pain becomes suddenly unbearable.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; chest X-ray is required to confirm the extent of the consolidation.
Clinical Course: The fever and chest pain should begin to significantly improve within 48-72 hours of starting the antibiotics. The cough and fatigue, however, may persist for several weeks as the lung slowly clears the debris.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your lung is currently filled with infectious fluid and your oxygen levels are borderline. If you find yourself struggling to complete a sentence without pausing for breath, or if you feel severe dizziness, you must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A bacterial infection has settled deeply into the bottom left lobe of your lung, turning it solid with pus and inflammation. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria, but your body needs to physically cough up the dead material. Cough suppressants trap this infection inside you and must be stopped immediately.
Actionable Steps: Start the antibiotics immediately. Take regular Paracetamol to reduce the fever and chest pain so you can breathe deeply. Sleep propped up on several pillows to make breathing easier.

